# Cornix module Kconfig.
#
# Symbols defined here are this repository's own ("CORNIX_" prefix, so they
# never collide with ZMK's ZMK_* or Zephyr's).  They are reachable from a
# board .conf under config/ because zephyr/module.yml declares
# `build: kconfig: Kconfig`.

config CORNIX_BLE_PAUSE_ON_USB
	bool "Pause the BLE host link while the USB endpoint is in use"
	default n
	depends on ZMK_BLE && ZMK_USB && (ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL || !ZMK_SPLIT)
	# The resume path calls zmk_ble_set_device_name() with the name that is
	# already set, which is the only public ZMK API that stops advertising
	# *and* re-runs update_advertising() (zmk/app/src/ble.c:358-376).  That
	# needs bt_set_name() to succeed, i.e. a dynamic device name.  ZMK only
	# `imply`s it (zmk/app/Kconfig:167), so make it a hard requirement here.
	select BT_DEVICE_NAME_DYNAMIC
	help
	  On a split keyboard the central half owns both the USB connection and
	  the BLE connection to the host.  When the keyboard is used over USB all
	  day, the BLE host (for example a Mac that keeps a bond for the
	  keyboard) still tries to reconnect, and every one of those attempts
	  costs the central radio time it needs for the split link to the other
	  half.  A host that fails to reconnect - a pairing/encryption failure it
	  retries about once a second - can starve a weak split link badly enough
	  to drop keystrokes.

	  With this enabled, the central disconnects the active BLE profile and
	  stops advertising as soon as the *selected* output endpoint is USB, and
	  starts advertising again as soon as it is not (a BLE host becomes the
	  selected endpoint, or the USB cable is unplugged).  The split link is
	  never touched: the central talks to its peripherals as a BLE *central*,
	  while this only stops the central's own peripheral-role advertising and
	  the one connection that belongs to the active host profile.

	  Asking for BLE with `&out OUT_BLE` still works: whenever the preferred
	  transport becomes BLE while USB is plugged in, the gate opens for
	  CORNIX_BLE_PAUSE_ON_USB_BLE_GRACE_MS so a host can connect.  If one
	  does, it becomes the selected endpoint and the gate stays open; if none
	  does, the preference is put back to USB (which ZMK persists) and the
	  gate closes again.

	  Costs while USB is selected: the BLE host cannot connect at all, so OS
	  detection over BLE and ZMK Studio over BLE are unavailable until USB is
	  deselected or unplugged.

config CORNIX_BLE_PAUSE_ON_USB_DELAY_MS
	int "Settle time in ms before the gate acts"
	depends on CORNIX_BLE_PAUSE_ON_USB
	default 500
	help
	  How long to wait after an endpoint / USB / BLE profile event before
	  pausing or resuming.  It debounces the burst of events that a replug or
	  an `&out` toggle produces, and - more importantly - it is also the
	  delay used to re-check advertising after ZMK restarts it from its own
	  disconnected() callback (zmk/app/src/ble.c:548).

config CORNIX_BLE_PAUSE_ON_USB_BLE_GRACE_MS
	int "BLE grace window in ms after the preferred transport becomes BLE"
	depends on CORNIX_BLE_PAUSE_ON_USB
	default 20000
	help
	  How long advertising stays allowed after the user asks for BLE while
	  USB is plugged in - `&out OUT_BLE`, the output toggle, or a preferred
	  transport restored from settings at boot.

	  It is needed because ZMK falls back from a preferred-but-not-ready BLE
	  to USB (zmk/app/src/endpoints.c, get_selected_transport()): while this
	  feature holds BLE down, the selected transport is USB whatever the user
	  asked for, so without a window a "gate when selected is USB" rule would
	  make `&out OUT_BLE` a no-op.

	  If no host connects within the window, the preferred transport is set
	  back to USB - ZMK persists it, so a stale preferred = BLE (pressed once
	  to test, then restored on every boot) can no longer disable the gate
	  forever - and the gate closes.  Make it long enough for the host to
	  notice the advertising and finish reconnecting; 20 s covers a Mac
	  waking up its Bluetooth stack.

config CORNIX_CRYPTO_SELFTEST
	bool "Run a PSA crypto self-test (P-256/ECDH, AES-CMAC, RNG) after boot"
	default n
	# PSA_CRYPTO_CLIENT is Zephyr's promptless "a PSA crypto API provider is
	# enabled in the system" symbol, and its help text says in so many words
	# that it is what lets a user "select desired PSA_WANT features"
	# (zephyr/modules/mbedtls/Kconfig.psa.auto:8-16).  It is exactly the
	# condition this test needs, and it is reached from both directions that
	# matter here:
	#   * a Bluetooth build: BT_ECC (pulled in by LE Secure Connections)
	#     selects MBEDTLS_PSA_CRYPTO_C, which enables MBEDTLS_PSA_CRYPTO_CLIENT
	#     -> PSA_CRYPTO_CLIENT (Kconfig.tls-generic:559-563), and BT_ECC/
	#     BT_CRYPTO already select every PSA_WANT_* symbol below;
	#   * a build without Bluetooth (native_sim): plain
	#     CONFIG_MBEDTLS_PSA_CRYPTO_C=y is enough.
	# Depending on MBEDTLS_PSA_CRYPTO_C directly instead makes Kconfig report a
	# dependency loop, because the PSA_WANT_* symbols selected below feed back
	# into the mbedTLS configuration; depending on PSA_CRYPTO_CLIENT - the same
	# condition their own `if` block uses - does not.
	# LOG is a dependency, not a `select`: selecting it from a symbol that
	# itself depends on the mbedTLS configuration closes a Kconfig dependency
	# loop through LOG's own reverse dependencies.  The test is pointless
	# without a log anyway - its whole output is LOG_INF/LOG_ERR lines.
	depends on PSA_CRYPTO_CLIENT && LOG
	select PSA_WANT_ALG_ECDH
	select PSA_WANT_ECC_SECP_R1_256
	select PSA_WANT_KEY_TYPE_ECC_KEY_PAIR_BASIC
	select PSA_WANT_KEY_TYPE_ECC_KEY_PAIR_IMPORT
	select PSA_WANT_KEY_TYPE_ECC_KEY_PAIR_EXPORT
	select PSA_WANT_KEY_TYPE_ECC_KEY_PAIR_GENERATE
	select PSA_WANT_ALG_CMAC
	select PSA_WANT_KEY_TYPE_AES
	help
	  Debug aid for LE Secure Connections pairing failures.

	  When LE SC pairing ends with `Security failed ... err 1`
	  (BT_SECURITY_ERR_AUTH_FAIL), the host has rejected our DHKey check or
	  confirm value (zephyr/subsys/bluetooth/host/smp.c, security_err_get()
	  maps BT_SMP_ERR_DHKEY_CHECK_FAILED and BT_SMP_ERR_CONFIRM_FAILED to
	  AUTH_FAIL).  Both are computed from the ECDH shared secret and AES-CMAC,
	  so the first question is whether this build's crypto is correct at all -
	  a question a toolchain or PSA driver difference can decide.

	  This runs the very same PSA Crypto calls the Bluetooth host uses
	  (zephyr/subsys/bluetooth/host/ecc.c: psa_import_key / psa_generate_key /
	  psa_export_public_key / psa_raw_key_agreement(PSA_ALG_ECDH, ...);
	  zephyr/subsys/bluetooth/crypto/bt_crypto_psa.c: psa_mac_compute with
	  PSA_ALG_CMAC) against published test vectors - RFC 5903 section 8.1 for
	  P-256 / ECDH and RFC 4493 section 4 for AES-CMAC - plus a random key pair
	  round trip and a non-constant check on psa_generate_random().

	  Each check logs one line

	      crypto selftest: <name> PASS|FAIL (details)

	  followed by `crypto selftest: ALL PASS` or `crypto selftest: FAILED n`.

	  Costs about 4 KiB of RAM (its own thread stack) and a few KiB of flash
	  while enabled; leave it off in normal builds.

config CORNIX_CRYPTO_SELFTEST_DELAY_MS
	int "Delay in ms between boot and the self-test"
	depends on CORNIX_CRYPTO_SELFTEST
	default 2000
	help
	  The test itself only needs psa_crypto_init(), which Zephyr's mbedTLS
	  module runs from a POST_KERNEL SYS_INIT
	  (zephyr/modules/mbedtls/zephyr_init.c, CONFIG_MBEDTLS_INIT), so it is
	  ready before the APPLICATION-level SYS_INIT that starts the test thread.
	  The delay exists for the *log*: with the USB CDC-ACM console the host has
	  to enumerate the port first, and deferred logging only starts draining
	  after CONFIG_LOG_PROCESS_THREAD_STARTUP_DELAY_MS.  0 runs the test
	  immediately.

config CORNIX_CRYPTO_SELFTEST_STACK_SIZE
	int "Stack size of the self-test thread"
	depends on CORNIX_CRYPTO_SELFTEST
	default 4096
	help
	  P-256 scalar multiplication is run on a dedicated thread rather than on
	  the system work queue for the same reason the Bluetooth host runs it on
	  its own long work queue (CONFIG_BT_LONG_WQ, 1400 bytes there): it needs
	  far more stack than CONFIG_SYSTEM_WORKQUEUE_STACK_SIZE leaves free.  The
	  default is generous because the generic mbedTLS ECP path needs much more
	  than the p256-m driver.

config CORNIX_REMOTE_BOOT
	bool "Enter the bootloader from the host over the USB CDC-ACM console"
	default n
	# The whole feature is a callback of the legacy USB device stack's CDC ACM
	# class driver, so without that class there is nothing to hook.  On a debug
	# image the class is there because CONFIG_ZMK_USB_LOGGING selects
	# USB_CDC_ACM (zmk/app/Kconfig).
	depends on USB_CDC_ACM
	# zephyr/subsys/usb/device/class/Kconfig.cdc:55 - it is what compiles
	# cdc_acm_dte_rate_callback_set() and the SET_LINE_CODING hook
	# (cdc_acm.c:182-186, 724-737).  It defaults to y only for BOOTLOADER_BOSSA
	# boards, so it has to be asked for here.
	select CDC_ACM_DTE_RATE_CALLBACK_SUPPORT
	# sys_reboot(); ZMK already defaults it to y (zmk/app/Kconfig:676) but this
	# code also builds outside a ZMK application.
	select REBOOT
	help
	  The "1200 bps touch": the host opens the CDC-ACM console port, sets the
	  line-coding baud rate to a magic value and closes it again.  No data is
	  exchanged, so any serial library can do it - see `scripts/flash.py
	  --enter left|right|reset-left` and the "Remote bootloader entry" section
	  of scripts/FLASHING.md.

	  Magic rates, all of them useless for a real console:

	    1200 - reboot *this* half into its UF2 bootloader, through the very
	           same `bootload` behavior the `&bootloader` key runs;
	    2400 - ask split peripheral CORNIX_REMOTE_BOOT_PERIPHERAL_INDEX (the
	           right half) to do the same.  ZMK's split transport invokes a
	           behavior on a peripheral *by name*, and reset.dtsi is compiled
	           into every ZMK build, so the peripheral needs no firmware change
	           and no USB stack of its own;
	    4800 - sys_reboot(SYS_REBOOT_COLD) of this half.

	  Any other rate is ignored, so opening the port with a terminal program
	  cannot reboot the keyboard by accident.

	  This is a debugging/flashing aid: anything that can open the console port
	  can put the keyboard into its bootloader, so enable it on debug images
	  (the `cornix-remote-boot` snippet, also pulled in by `cornix-debug-log`)
	  rather than on a daily-driver build.

config CORNIX_REMOTE_BOOT_DELAY_MS
	int "Delay in ms between the magic baud rate and the reboot"
	depends on CORNIX_REMOTE_BOOT
	default 100
	help
	  The rate callback runs in the USB device stack's context and has to
	  return before the control transfer can be ACKed, so the action is run
	  from a delayable work item instead.  The delay also gives the host time
	  to finish closing the port - a device that vanishes mid-close leaves a
	  stuck handle behind on Windows - and debounces hosts that send
	  SetLineCoding twice.

config CORNIX_REMOTE_BOOT_PERIPHERAL_INDEX
	int "Split peripheral index the 2400 bps trigger targets"
	depends on CORNIX_REMOTE_BOOT
	default 0
	help
	  Cornix has exactly one peripheral (the right half), which is index 0.
	  The index is the `source` a peripheral key event would carry, i.e. the
	  slot in zmk/app/src/split/bluetooth/central.c's `peripherals[]`.

config CORNIX_APP_SWITCH
	bool "macOS-style application switcher behavior (zmk,behavior-cornix-app-switch)"
	default y
	# The behavior drives the HID modifier state (zmk_hid_masked_modifiers_set)
	# and raises keycode events, both of which ZMK only builds for a non-split
	# board or the central half - zmk/app/CMakeLists.txt:48 guards src/hid.c,
	# src/hid_listener.c and every keyboard behavior with exactly this
	# condition.  On the right half the `app_tab` node is still in the
	# devicetree (the keymap is shared) but nothing compiles against it, which
	# is also what happens to `zmk,behavior-mod-morph` there.
	depends on ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL || !ZMK_SPLIT
	help
	  Compiles src/behavior_app_switch.c, the `zmk,behavior-cornix-app-switch`
	  behavior used by `app_tab` on the Win layer of config/cornix.keymap
	  (position 0, Tab).

	  While the left thumb rest key (position 41, `mod-position`) holds Ctrl
	  (`hold-mod`), a press hides that Ctrl from the HID report, holds Alt
	  (`switch-mod`) and taps Tab; every further press taps Tab again with Alt
	  still down, so the Windows Alt+Tab switcher stays open and steps through
	  the window list exactly like Cmd+Tab does on macOS.  Releasing 41
	  releases Alt, which is what commits the selection.  Pressed without Ctrl
	  held the key is a plain `&kp TAB`.

	  This replaces a `zmk,behavior-mod-morph` to `&kp LA(TAB)`, which could
	  not work: LALT is an *implicit* modifier of that keypress and ZMK
	  releases implicit modifiers together with the key
	  (zmk/app/src/hid_listener.c, zmk_hid_implicit_modifiers_release()), so
	  the switcher closed on every tap.

	  The source is additionally gated on DT_HAS_COMPAT_STATUS_OKAY(), so with
	  the symbol on but no node using the compatible it compiles to nothing;
	  leaving it at its default therefore costs a keymap that does not use it
	  no flash at all.
